<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  RQFlinch - the "shrug it off" hit reaction.

  Selected instead of the vanilla reaction whenever the RQNoFlinch animation
  variable is true on the zombie. RQFlinch.lua (client) owns that variable and
  RQPoise decides when to set it; the variable name is defined in one place in
  RQFlinch.lua and MUST match the condition below. A mismatch fails silently -
  this node simply never wins and flinches look normal.

  WHY A NODE AND NOT A TRANSITION. We cannot stop the zombie entering the
  hitreaction state: those transitions live in media/actiongroups, which
  ActionGroup.load() reads from the base install only (getMediaFile plus
  listFiles), with no mod path. AnimSets ARE mod-loadable - AnimationSet:54
  goes through resolveAllDirectories -> walkGameAndModFiles. So we let the
  state be entered and make it cost nothing.

  WHY m_ConditionPriority. AnimNode.compareSelectionConditions (:287-301)
  orders by abstract-ness, then m_ConditionPriority, then condition COUNT.
  Winning on count alone would break the day vanilla adds a condition to a
  sibling node, so the priority is stated outright. 100 is far above anything
  in the vanilla zombie set, which leaves all of them unmodified.

  m_SpeedScale is what makes this work: the reaction animation still plays, at
  twenty times speed, so it is over inside a frame or two and the zombie is
  back to acting. It reads as a twitch rather than as nothing happening, which
  is the intent - the Boss should look like it noticed and did not care.
-->
<animNode>
	<m_Name>RQFlinchSuppressed</m_Name>
	<m_AnimName>Zombie_ShotChestL</m_AnimName>
	<m_Looped>false</m_Looped>
	<m_BlendTime>0.0</m_BlendTime>
	<m_BlendOutTime>0.0</m_BlendOutTime>
	<m_SpeedScale>20.0</m_SpeedScale>
	<m_ConditionPriority>100</m_ConditionPriority>
	<m_Conditions x_name="rqf1a2b3-0001-4000-8000-rftddirge0001">
		<m_Name>RQNoFlinch</m_Name>
		<m_Type>BOOL</m_Type>
		<m_Value>true</m_Value>
	</m_Conditions>
</animNode>
